{"reference":"Ruth 1","book":"Ruth","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Places and movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter begins in Bethlehem in Judah, with Judah named as the setting. The designation identifies Bethlehem’s location; it does not itself make a claim about tribal ownership. The family leaves this setting for Moab during the famine, and Naomi and Ruth later return to Bethlehem, framing the chapter around departure and return rather than supplying a detailed travel route.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1-2","Ruth 1:19-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_a87c7e03071e33fd8da29908","reader_path_ruth_001_a0020b0b024377bc9ed4b7fc","reader_path_ruth_001_504abb4b5ff182dcf2887036"]}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Ruth’s pledge","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Ruth’s response to Naomi is a commitment of lasting solidarity: she promises to go and live where Naomi goes, to share Naomi’s people and God, and to remain with her until death. The words explain why Ruth does not follow Orpah’s return home; her decision is not merely to continue the journey but to bind her future to Naomi’s.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:16-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_ba143a52c924176dd3d46f0e"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A question readers debate","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Ruth continues to be identified as a Moabite even as she pledges herself to Naomi, Naomi’s people, and Naomi’s God. Some interpreters understand this feature as a direct response to a particular later policy about intermarriage, but that proposal remains debated; the chapter itself does not settle the question.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1","Ruth 1:6","Ruth 1:16-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_3c0572b2558deeb811c683b5"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The story beyond this chapter","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Chapter 1 opens Ruth’s larger movement from famine, bereavement, and Naomi’s sense of emptiness toward harvest, kinship redemption, birth, communal blessing, and genealogy. The return to Bethlehem at the beginning of barley harvest therefore points forward within the story rather than resolving Naomi’s loss at once.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 3:1-18","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_b5bf7ef03c21537b1fe1dbfe"]},{"id":"block_5","text":"Later canonical discussion of Ammonites and Moabites includes an assembly exclusion applied in Nehemiah 13. Reading that material alongside Ruth requires attention to Ruth the Moabite and to texts that welcome faithful foreigners, rather than treating Ruth’s story as support for a universal ethnic ban.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 3:1-18","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_1ea3e942e71e422247c79172"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}